  <!-- ── Why This Matters ── -->
  <h2>Why This Matters</h2>
  <ul class="insult-list">
    <li>Light spectrum, timing, and intensity dictates melatonin production, cortisol curves, testosterone release timing, and metabolic rate throughout the day</li>
    <li>Standard LED and fluorescent bulbs emit a blue-dominant spectrum that tells your brain it's high noon — at 10pm — suppressing melatonin and degrading sleep architecture</li>
    <li>Most men indoors all morning never get the full-spectrum sunlight that anchors the circadian clock — the result is a system running with no strong signal at either end</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════
       HERITAGE INCANDESCENT FLAGSHIP SECTION
  ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="heritage-section" aria-label="Heritage Incandescent Bulb Flagship">
    <div class="heritage-badge">FLAGSHIP · HERITAGE LINE</div>

    <h2 class="heritage-title">The Heritage Bulb — Full-Spectrum, Zero-Flicker, Engineered for Biology</h2>

    <div class="heritage-science">
      <p>
        Edison's incandescent bulb is — by physics — still the most biologically aligned artificial light source ever invented.
        Unlike modern LEDs, which emit a narrow spike of blue-dominant wavelengths, an incandescent filament glows with
        <strong>continuous blackbody radiation</strong> across the entire visible and near-infrared spectrum.
        That means a color rendering index of 100, zero flicker (no 60 Hz to 4 kHz strobe effect on your retina),
        rich red and near-infrared content that feeds mitochondrial function, and smooth analog dimming — the light dims
        warmer and richer, the way firelight does, not the way a switching power supply does.
        Modern efficiency legislation pushed lumens-per-watt as the sole optimization target, trading biology for a kilowatt-hour metric.
      </p>
      <p>
        The good news: specialty incandescents are still legal. The 2023 DOE efficiency rule (45 lumens/watt minimum) ended
        general-service incandescents — but explicitly exempts <strong>rough-service</strong> and vibration-service bulbs,
        heat lamps, appliance bulbs, and certain industrial categories. Rough-service incandescents are rated at 130V and
        run on your household 120V supply. The voltage headroom means the filament runs cooler, lasts far longer, and is
        physically more robust. The result: <strong>10,000–20,000 hours of life</strong> — 10 to 20 times the standard
        incandescent — sourced from verified specialty manufacturers, curated for circadian biology. We call this the
        My4MLife Heritage Bulb line.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Why Edison's Bulb Still Wins — Comparison Block ── -->
